{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And Elohim saw all that He had made, and behold, it was very good.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): He mentioned 'very good' concerning the whole, whereas until now, in regard to the particulars brought into being each day, He mentioned only 'ki tov (that it was good),' because the whole is more excellent than the part. And behold, there are three grades: 'ki tov (good),' 'and behold, it was very good,' and 'tov (good).'" "<b>And know</b> and understand that every existent being in the world stands by the power of another existent; it cannot stand on its own without another. Suppose, for example, you say that in all the world there is only one species, and it is living — if there is no earth, that living species cannot subsist on its own. Or if there is earth but no heavens, it cannot endure, for it is through the motion of the heavens that the earth is sustained. If so, the entire world and everything upon it cannot have one existent separated from another, for all are linked one to another, the upper with the lower, and all is one — as we have received the proof from the word אחד (one), that all of reality is entirely one. And just as the created being is one, so the Creator is none other than one. From this you learn that all existent beings in the world are bound one to another; no existent can be complete without its fellow — just as it is impossible for a hand or a foot to move on its own without the rest of the body's limbs, as when it is whole. And indeed, man has been called by the investigative sages 'a small world,' and the world is likewise called by them 'a great man,' to indicate that everything is linked and unified. This is the statement of the prophet (Isaiah 48:13): ״קורא אני אלהים יעמדו יחדו״ — and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: what is 'yachdav (together)'? That they should not destroy one another. Therefore every existent on its own exists in potentiality, and for this reason He mentioned regarding the particulars created each day 'ki tov (that it was good)'; but together with all the rest of the existents it exists in actuality, and for this reason He mentioned regarding the whole 'very good,' because the whole is more complete than the part. And you will find the same in Moshe Rabbeinu, peace be upon him: because he existed complete in potentiality, Scripture mentioned of him 'ki tov (that he was good),' as it says (Exodus 2:2): ״ותרא אותו כי טוב הוא״ — and when his intellect emerged from potentiality into actuality, Scripture mentioned of him 'me'od (very),' as it is written (Numbers 12:3): ״והאיש משה ענו מאד״. 'Tov (good)' is the highest grade of all, and there is no true Good except the Holy One, blessed be He, as it says (Psalms 145:9): ״טוב ה' לכל״ — but as for what is written, ״הודו לה' כי טוב״, the meaning is 'for He has done good with us.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'and behold, it was good' — this is the good impulse (yetzer tov); 'very (me'od)' — this is the evil impulse (yetzer hara) and the attribute of punishment. For were it not for the evil impulse, no man would build a house, nor marry a wife, nor beget children, and the world would be desolate. And they further expounded: 'and behold, it was very good' — this is death, for death is the cause through which the generations are renewed and come into being. Therefore they enacted in the house of mourning to say 'HaTov veHaMeitiv (the Good and the One who does good).' And there is no doubt that death is good for the intention of HaShem, blessed be He, for it is life to the soul of the righteous, as it is written (Psalms 116:15): ״יקר בעיני ה' המותה לחסידיו״ — and the addition of the letter hei (in המותה, instead of המות) comes to clarify that the death of the righteous is not through the destroying angel but through the Shekhinah, which is alluded to by the hei — in the manner of the added hei in the matter of the descent of Yaakov our father to Egypt, which is (Genesis 46:4): ״אנכי ארד עמך מצרימה ואנכי אעלך גם עלה״ — He hinted to him that the Shekhinah would be with him in his descent to Egypt and also in his ascent." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): 'and behold, it was very good.' Know that the language of 'making' (עשיה) applies to the work of nature, such as the creation of the spheres and all that is beneath them, which are included within the domain of nature. Therefore, at the seal of the work of creation, which is the work of nature, He employed the term וירא (and He saw), which is a term of providence, and He joined to it the term 'making' (עשיה), to indicate that He, blessed be He, exercises providence over the spheres and that they are the work of His hands. And He added for you an explanation by way of allusion: that the spheres are nine, according to the words of the investigative sages. This you find in the word והנ״ה (והנה), whose numerical value is that of גלג״ל (galgal, 'sphere'), and this is 'the sphere of the intellect' (גלגל השכל). And he further explained: the sphere of the intellect moves the ninth sphere, which is the encompassing sphere, called Aravot; and the encompassing sphere moves the eight spheres within it. This is what טו״ב (tov, 'good') alludes to. Therefore He sealed this natural world with the words והנ״ה טו״ב מאד — whose meaning is: the sphere of the intellect (גלגל השכל) moves the ninth sphere (the encompassing one), and the ninth sphere moves the eight within it. And in the word מא״ד (me'od, 'very'), which He added, whose numerical value is מ״ה (= 45), the matter was to inform you of the measure of the stature of the nine spheres — that each one of them, according to the opinion of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, has a thickness of five hundred years. And understand this." "<b>The sixth day.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the addition of the letter hei in 'הששי (the sixth)' — unlike the rest of the days — is all for the preeminence of the first man and for his honor, for he was created on it, and with him the entire work was completed; he was the purpose of creation and its seal and essence. Therefore He said expressly 'the sixth day,' meaning the known and celebrated one. And yet another intention is included therein, for He revealed to us by this that during all six days the matters were quintupled — five in each and every day — and therefore the sixth day was sealed with a hei to allude to this. And how is this so? For on the first day we find five lights; on the second day, five firmaments and five occurrences of water; on the third day, five species of vegetation; on the fourth day, five kinds of luminaries; on the fifth day, five kinds of motion found in every species of living creature; on the sixth day, in which man was created — five senses of his body, five motions of his soul, and the five names that a living soul possesses. From this you learn that all the affairs of the six days of creation are quintupled, five in each and every day, and therefore He sealed the sixth day with the letter hei —" "<b>And you should</b> reflect on all the creatures of the six days: why each and every thing was singled out to be created on the day it was created, and why the day on which light was created is called 'one day' (יום אחד), and the day on which the firmament was created 'second day' (יום שני), and the day on which dry land appeared 'third day' (יום שלישי), and so for all of them. The day on which light was created is called 'one day' (יום אחד), because light is an allusion to unity; and you already know that He is the King of Glory, and this is what is written (Psalms 27:1): ״ה' אורי וישעי״ — and there were two lights, one primordial and one renewed; understand this. The day on which the firmament was created is called 'second day' (יום שני), because it is an allusion to duality, and it was created to separate and divide between the upper waters and the lower waters — and behold, they are two opposites, this an upper part and this a lower part, and there is no greater change than this; therefore it is called 'second day.' The day on which dry land appeared is called 'third day,' because on it the form of the world was seen, divided into three parts: a third settled, a third wilderness, a third sea — and behold, it is tripartite in its creation. The day on which the luminaries were created is called 'fourth,' because the luminaries change four times: in a day, in four watches; in a month, in four weeks; in a year, in four seasons. The day on which the possessors of the moving soul were created is called 'fifth,' because the movements of every living soul are five. The day on which man was created is called 'sixth,' because on it man stood in all six directions; therefore the day on which he was created is called 'the sixth day' (יום הששי)." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> 'The sixth day' — the Holy One, blessed be He, made a condition with the works of Creation: if Israel accepts the Torah, well and good; and if not, I return you to chaos and void. As it is said: ״יום הששי״ — these are the five books of the Torah, which were given on the sixth of Sivan." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> ״יום הששי״ — He sealed the work of the sixth with the letter ה, and it is the first letter of the Name by which the intention of the prayers in Rosh HaShanah is set. And this sixth day was the twenty-fifth of Elul, on which the world was created, and the day of Rosh HaShanah was the day on which the first man was judged, and so it is the day of judgment for all his descendants after him. And there is further to explain that the word הששי does not refer back to 'day' — for if so it should have said היום הששי — but it is construct: יום הששי, a day of 'the sixth,' that is, the day of the sixth Name, and it is the sixth seal of the six seals by which the world was sealed for the six directions. And understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {1 "Rabbeinu Bahya cites Isaiah 48:13 but quotes only the phrase 'קורא אני אלהים יעמדו יחדו' — the full verse reads differently; rendered exactly as he quotes it. The citation 'טוב ה׳ לכל' is normalized to Psalms 145:9; 'הודו לה׳ כי טוב' (from Psalm 136:1) is quoted without a citation by the author."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:29:27.444911711Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}